eor:satellite.1960-006A a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "MIDAS 2"@en ;
    schema:alternateName "FTV 1007"@en,
        "Midas 2"@en,
        "Midas ETS-I F2"@en,
        "Missile Defense Alarm System 2"@en ;
    schema:name "MIDAS 2"@en ;
    mac:description """GCMD definitions: The MIDAS 2 (Missile Defense Alarm System) satellite was an earth-orbiting
satellite designed to measure IR background and define IR sources.  In
addition, the satellite carried experiments to measure cosmic radiation,
atmospheric density, thermal emission and reflected solar radiation from the
earth, and micrometeorites.  A plasma probe was included too.   The spacecraft
was chemical-battery powered.  IR radiation data were received for the lifetime
of the battery pack, which powered the final transmission on May 26, 1960.
__________
Taken from the NSSDC System for Information Retrieval and Storage (SIRS).  For
more information contact the NSSDC Coordinated Request and User Support Office,
301-286-6695 (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, Code 933.4, Greenbelt, Maryland
20771, USA)."""@en ;
    smi:inclination "33.05"^^xsd:float ;
    smi:period "87.82"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:COSPARId "1960-006A" ;
    eo-ont:EOLDate "1974-02-07"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:NORADId 43 ;
    eo-ont:apogee "168.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:dryMass "1410.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchDate "1960-05-24"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:launchMass "2000.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchSite eor:launch_site.AFETR ;
    eo-ont:operationalStatus eor:operational_status.decayed ;
    eo-ont:orbitType eor:orbit_type.LLEO_I ;
    eo-ont:owner eor:owner.US ;
    eo-ont:perigee "165.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:relatedSatId "GCMD:cc93fc95-4b03-4d67-ab48-8216434a8944" ;
    eo-ont:satelliteAgency "AFBMD"@en ;
    eo-ont:webInfo ""^^xsd:anyURI .

eor:satellite.1960-016A a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "TIROS 2"@en ;
    schema:alternateName ":N"@en,
        ":NA"@en,
        "NASA A-2"@en,
        "TIROS-2"@en,
        "Television Infrared Observation Satellite-2"@en,
        "Television and Infra-Red Observation Satellite - 2"@en,
        "Television and InfraRed Observation Satellite"@en,
        "Tiros 2"@en,
        "Tiros B:NB"@en ;
    schema:name "TIROS 2"@en ;
    mac:description "CEOS applications: To test the experimental television techniques and infrared equipment designed to develop a worldwide meteorological satellite information system. To evaluate a new attitude control system for spacecraft orientation which utilizes the Earth's magnetic field."@en,
        """GCMD definitions: Objectives: To test the experimental television techniques and infrared equipment designed to develop a worldwide meteorological satellite information system. To evaluate a new attitude control system for spacecraft orientation which utilizes the Earth's magnetic field.

Description: The spacecraft was 42 inches in diameter, 19 inches high and weighed 280 pounds. The craft was made of aluminum alloy and stainless steel which was then covered by 9260 solar cells. The solar cells served to charge the nicad batteries. Two television cameras were housed in the craft, one low-resolution and one high-resolution. A magnetic tape recorder for each camera was supplied for storing photographs while the satellite was out of range of the ground station network. In addition, an infrared horizon sensor for attitude control, a direction indicator for picture orientation, two infrared radiation experiments, and a magnetic orientation control experiment were included.

The spacecraft was 42 inches in diameter, 19 inches high and weighed 280 pounds. The craft was made of aluminum alloy and stainless steel which was then covered by 9260 solar cells. The solar cells served to charge the nicad batteries. Two television cameras were housed in the craft, one low-resolution and one high-resolution. A magnetic tape recorder for each camera was supplied for storing photographs while the satellite was out of range of the ground station network. In addition, an infrared horizon sensor for attitude control, a direction indicator for picture orientation, two infrared radiation experiments, and a magnetic orientation control experiment were included.

The antennas consisted of four rods from the base plate to serve as transmitters and one vertical rod from the center of the top plate to serve as a receiver. The video systems relayed thousands of pictures containing cloud-cover views of the Earth. Early photographs provided information concerning the structure of large-scale cloud regimes. In addition, the experiment to partially control the orientation of the satellite spin axis was successful, as was the experiment with infrared sensors."""@en,
        "OSCAR description: 2nd flight unit of the TIROS programme. Main mission: cloud imagery. Substantial contribution to radiation budget study."@en ;
    smi:inclination "48.44"^^xsd:float ;
    smi:period "87.72"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:COSPARId "1960-016A" ;
    eo-ont:EOLDate "2014-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:NORADId 63 ;
    eo-ont:apogee "163.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:dryMass "122.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchDate "1960-11-23"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:launchMass "122.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchSite eor:launch_site.AFETR ;
    eo-ont:operationalStatus eor:operational_status.decayed ;
    eo-ont:orbitAltitude "670.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:orbitType eor:orbit_type.LEO_I ;
    eo-ont:owner eor:owner.US ;
    eo-ont:perigee "160.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:radarCrossSection "0.878"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:relatedSatId "CEOS:897",
        "GCMD:abcc3bb4-8f36-4008-b12e-d29b028ecad9",
        "OSCAR:tiros_2" ;
    eo-ont:satelliteAgency "NASA"@en ;
    eo-ont:webInfo "https://science.nasa.gov/missions/tiros"^^xsd:anyURI .

eor:satellite.1961-017A a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "TIROS 3"@en ;
    schema:alternateName ":N"@en,
        ":NA"@en,
        "NASA A-3"@en,
        "TIROS-3"@en,
        "Television Infrared Observation Satellite-3"@en,
        "Television and Infra-Red Observation Satellite - 3"@en,
        "Television and InfraRed Observation Satellite"@en,
        "Tiros 3"@en,
        "Tiros C:NB"@en ;
    schema:name "TIROS 3"@en ;
    mac:description "CEOS applications: Continued development of the experimental television techniques and infrared equipment leading to a worldwide meteorological information system. To obtain infrared measurements of the solar energy absorbed, reflected and emitted by the Earth."@en,
        """GCMD definitions: Objectives: Continued development of the experimental television techniques and infrared equipment leading to a worldwide meteorological information system. To obtain infrared measurements of the solar energy absorbed, reflected and emitted by the Earth.

Description: The spacecraft was 42 inches in diameter, 19 inches high and weighed 285 pounds. The craft was made of aluminum alloy and stainless steel then covered by 9260 solar cells. The solar cells served to charge the nickel-cadmium batteries. Three major changes were made from the previous TIROS models. Two wide-angle television cameras were housed in the craft in place of one high-resolution and one low-resolution camera.

A new infrared experiment and improved remote control programmers were also new additions. This craft contained an electronic clock to control the operations of the infrared horizon sensor as well as the magnetic orientation system. A magnetic tape recorder was still provided for each camera to store photographs while the satellite was out of range of the ground station network. One scanning and two non-scanning radiometers were also on board.

The antennas were of the same configuration as both previous TIROS models. Although one of the cameras failed 12 days into the mission, photograph quality from the other camera was excellent and many tropical storms during the 1961 hurricane season were photographed. TIROS-3 was also credited with the discovery of Hurricane Esther."""@en,
        "OSCAR description: 3rd flight unit of the TIROS programme. Main mission: cloud imagery. Substantial contribution to radiation budget study."@en ;
    smi:inclination "47.9"^^xsd:float ;
    smi:period "99.64"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:COSPARId "1961-017A" ;
    eo-ont:EOLDate "1962-02-27"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:NORADId 162 ;
    eo-ont:apogee "773.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:dryMass "129.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchDate "1961-07-12"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:launchMass "129.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchSite eor:launch_site.AFETR ;
    eo-ont:operationalStatus eor:operational_status.nonoperational ;
    eo-ont:orbitAltitude "780.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:orbitType eor:orbit_type.LEO_I ;
    eo-ont:owner eor:owner.US ;
    eo-ont:perigee "710.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:radarCrossSection "0.8244"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:relatedSatId "CEOS:898",
        "GCMD:0752330f-2d01-4129-89da-8544369206cb",
        "OSCAR:tiros_3" ;
    eo-ont:satelliteAgency "NASA"@en ;
    eo-ont:webInfo "https://science.nasa.gov/missions/tiros"^^xsd:anyURI .

eor:satellite.1962-002A a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "TIROS 4"@en ;
    schema:alternateName ":N"@en,
        ":NA"@en,
        "NASA A-9"@en,
        "TIROS-4"@en,
        "Television Infrared Observation Satellite-4"@en,
        "Television and Infra-Red Observation Satellite - 4"@en,
        "Television and InfraRed Observation Satellite"@en,
        "Tiros 4"@en,
        "Tiros D:NB"@en ;
    schema:name "TIROS 4"@en ;
    mac:description "CEOS applications: Continued research into and development of the meteorological satellite information system. This mission was designed to maintain an operational TIROS in orbit for an extended period of time and to obtain improved data for operational use in weather forecasting during the northern hemisphere hurricane season."@en,
        """GCMD definitions: Objectives: Continued research into and development of the meteorological satellite information system. This mission was designed to maintain an operational TIROS in orbit for an extended period of time and to obtain improved data for operational use in weather forecasting during the northern hemisphere hurricane season.

Description: The spacecraft was 42 inches in diameter, 19 inches high and weighed 285 pounds. The craft was made of aluminum alloy and stainless steel and was then covered by 9260 solar cells. The solar cells served to charge the 63 on-board batteries.

A new lens system was implemented for this launch. The lens was designed to reduce distortion and improve resolution. This craft also contained an electronic clock to control the operations of the infrared horizon sensor as well as the magnetic orientation control system. A magnetic tape recorder was still provided for each camera to store photographs while the satellite was out of range of the ground station network. One scanning and two non-scanning radiometers were also on board. The transmitting and receiving antennas were of the same configuration as the previous TIROS models.

TIROS-4 pictures were the best to date, allowing the US Weather Bureau to initiate an international facsimile transmission network in order to share the cloud pictures with weather services around the world."""@en,
        "OSCAR description: 4th flight unit of the TIROS programme. Main mission: cloud imagery. Substantial contribution to radiation budget study."@en ;
    smi:inclination "48.3"^^xsd:float ;
    smi:period "99.52"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:COSPARId "1962-002A" ;
    eo-ont:EOLDate "1962-07-19"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:NORADId 226 ;
    eo-ont:apogee "790.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:dryMass "129.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchDate "1962-02-08"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:launchMass "129.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchSite eor:launch_site.AFETR ;
    eo-ont:operationalStatus eor:operational_status.nonoperational ;
    eo-ont:orbitAltitude "770.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:orbitType eor:orbit_type.LEO_I ;
    eo-ont:owner eor:owner.US ;
    eo-ont:perigee "681.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:radarCrossSection "0.812"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:relatedSatId "CEOS:899",
        "GCMD:d6369522-f750-4946-9946-2d2ed6ce56b5",
        "OSCAR:tiros_4" ;
    eo-ont:satelliteAgency "NASA"@en ;
