eor:satellite.1964-076A a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "EXPLORER 24 (ADI-2)"@en ;
    schema:alternateName "00931"@en,
        "AD-B"@en,
        "Atmosphere Dynamics B (Explorer 24)"@en,
        "Explorer 24"@en ;
    schema:name "EXPLORER 24 (ADI-2)"@en ;
    mac:description """GCMD definitions: orer 24 was placed in orbit together with Explorer 25 from a single launch vehicle.  Explorer 24 was identical in configuration to the previously launched balloon satellites Explorer 9 and 19.  The spacecraft was 3.6 m in diameter, was built of alternating layers of aluminum foil and plastic film, and was covered uniformly with 5.1-cm white dots for thermal control.  It was designed to yield atmospheric density near perigee as a function of space and time from sequential observations of the sphere's position in orbit.  To facilitate ground tracking, the satellite carried a 136-MHz tracking beacon.  The satellite reentered the earth's atmosphere on October 18, 1968.

https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1964-076A


