eor:satellite.1974-039A a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "ATS 6"@en ;
    schema:alternateName "ATS F"@en,
        "ATS-6"@en,
        "Advanced Technology Satellite-6"@en,
        "Application Technology Satellite - 6"@en,
        "Applications Technology Satellite-6"@en ;
    schema:name "ATS 6"@en ;
    mac:description "GCMD definitions: ATS-6 (Applications Technology Satellite-6) was a NASA experimental satellite, built by Fairchild Space and Electronics Division. It was launched May 30, 1974, and decommissioned July 1979. At the time of launch, it was the most powerful telecommunication satellite in orbit. ATS-6 carried no fewer than 23 different experiments, and introduced several breakthroughs. It was the first 3-axis stabilized spacecraft in geostationary orbit. It was also the first to use experimentally with some success electric propulsion in geostationary orbit. It also carried several particle physics experiments, including the first heavy ion detector in geostationary orbit."@en,
        "OSCAR description: 3rd (last) flight unit of the ATS programme (considering that ATS-2, ATS-4 and ATS-5 failed to achieve the orbit). Main mission (tlc mission not included): cloud observation."@en,
        "OSOidx functionOfSpaceObject: Practical applications of space based technology."@en ;
