eor:satellite.2019-093E a eo-ont:Satellite ;
    rdfs:label "CBERS 4A"@en ;
    schema:alternateName "CBERS-4A"@en,
        "CBERS-4B"@en,
        "China Brazil Earth Resources Satellite - 4A"@en,
        "China-Brazil Earth Resources Satellite - 4B (ZY-1 FM4B) "@en,
        "China-Brazil Earth Resources Satellite 4A"@en,
        "China-Brazil Earth Resources Satellite-4A"@en,
        "ZY-1 04A"@en,
        "Zi Yuan 1-04A"@en ;
    schema:name "CBERS 4A"@en ;
    mac:description "CEOS applications: Earth resources, environmental monitoring, land surface."@en,
        """GCMD definitions: Launched in 2019, the China-Brazil Earth Resources Satellite-4A (CBERS-4A) is an optical imaging satellite collaboratively operated by the China Centre for Resources Satellite and Data Application (CRESDA) and the Instituto Nacional del Pesquisas Espacias (INPE) - Brazil’s National Institute of Space Research. CBERS-4A will be used to monitor water resources, agriculture, urban growth, and land use, with a large focus on the preservation of the Amazon rainforest.

Quick facts : Overview
Mission type: EO
Agency: CRESDA, INPE
Mission status: Operational (nominal)
Launch date: 20 Dec 2019
Measurement domain: Land
Measurement category: Multi-purpose imagery (land), Surface temperature (land), Vegetation, Albedo and reflectance, Landscape topography
Measurement detailed: Land surface imagery, Vegetation type, Earth surface albedo, Land surface temperature, Land surface topography
Instruments: DCS, WFI (CBERS-4A), WPM, MUX (CBERS-4A), IRMSS-2
Instrument type: maging multi-spectral radiometers (vis/IR), High resolution optical imagers, Data collection

Summary
Mission Capabilities

CBERS-4A has three imaging instruments on board:

    a high-resolution optical imager - Multispectral and Panchromatic Wide-Scan Camera (WPM);
    as well as two different multi-spectral radiometers, a Multispectral Camera (MUX) and a Wide Field Imager (WFI).

The data is used to monitor water resources, agriculture, urban growth and land use, with a large focus on controlling the deforestation and burning of the Amazon rainforest. The addition of CBERS-4A to the satellites observing Brazil is beneficial due to the high frequency of clouds in the Amazon region; the more satellites observing the area, the higher the chance of uninterrupted images. The data collected will be of large use to the national programs of Brazil, Prodes (measurement of deforestation by remote sensing) and Deter (Brazilian real-time deforestation detection).
Performance Specifications

All three instruments measure over three visible bands (blue, green, red) and one near infrared (NIR) band. WPM also observes over one panchromatic band with a spatial resolution of 2 m, and a resolution of 8 m for the visible and NIR bands. It has a swath width of 92 km. MUX has a resolution of 16 m and a swath width of 95 km, while WFI has a resolution of 55 m and a swath width of 684 km.

CBERS-4A undertakes a sun-synchronous orbit with an altitude of 629 km and an inclination of 97.9°. The satellite has a period of 97.3 minutes and a repeat cycle of 31 days.
Space and Hardware Components

The 1,730 kg satellite CBERS-4A was launched onboard a Long March 4B (LM-4B) launch vehicle from the Taiyuan Satellite Launch Complex in Shanxi, China."""@en,
        "OSCAR description: 6th (last) flight unit of the CBERS (ZY-1) programme. Main mission: land observation."@en,
        "OSOidx functionOfSpaceObject: Earth observation"@en,
        "UCS Purpose: Earth Observation "@en,
        "UCS Users: Government"@en,
        "UCS detailedPurpose: Optical Imaging"@en ;
    smi:inclination "97.79"^^xsd:float ;
    smi:period "97.19"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:COSPARId "2019-093E" ;
    eo-ont:NORADId 44883 ;
    eo-ont:apogee "627.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:dataPortal "http://www.dgi.inpe.br/CDSR/"^^xsd:anyURI,
        "http://www2.dgi.inpe.br/catalogo/explore"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    eo-ont:dryMass "1900.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:ect "10:30:00"^^xsd:time ;
    eo-ont:launchDate "2019-12-20"^^xsd:date ;
    eo-ont:launchMass "1980.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:launchSite eor:launch_site.TAISC ;
    eo-ont:operationalStatus eor:operational_status.operational ;
    eo-ont:orbitAltitude "629.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:orbitSense "Descending"@en ;
    eo-ont:orbitType eor:orbit_type.LEO_S ;
    eo-ont:owner eor:owner.CHBZ ;
    eo-ont:perigee "622.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:relatedSatId "CEOS:775",
        "GCMD:3c6f91fd-5abc-4007-ad96-cc585b38b1b1",
        "ITC:satellite-41",
        "OSCAR:cbers_4b" ;
    eo-ont:repeatCycle "31.0"^^xsd:float ;
    eo-ont:satelliteAgency "INPE, CRESDA"@en ;
    eo-ont:webInfo "eoPortal: https://www.eoportal.org/satellite-missions/cbers-4a#performance-specifications"^^xsd:anyURI,
        "http://www.cbers.inpe.br/"^^xsd:anyURI .

